154	<section>
155	<h2>1. An ADC Code Is a Ratio, Not a Voltage</h2>
156	
157	<p>The single most important idea in this lesson: <strong>the number an ADC returns has no voltage unit attached to it.</strong> A 12-bit ADC answers exactly one question — <em>"what fraction of the reference span is the input sitting at?"</em> — quantised into 4096 steps.</p>
158	
159	<p>The reference voltage is the <strong>unit of measure</strong>. Reading code <code>2047</code> tells you the input is at roughly half the reference — and nothing more. Half of <em>what</em> depends entirely on which reference was selected when the conversion ran:</p>
160	
161	<table>
162	  <tr><th>Code returned</th><th>Selected reference (VR+)</th><th>Actual input voltage</th></tr>
163	  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>1.2 V (internal VREF)</td><td>≈ 0.60 V</td></tr>
164	  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>2.5 V (internal VREF2.5)</td><td>≈ 1.25 V</td></tr>
165	  <tr><td><code>2047</code></td><td>3.3 V (AVCC)</td><td>≈ 1.65 V</td></tr>
166	</table>
167	
168	<p>Same code, three different physical voltages. Conversely, the <strong>same input voltage produces different codes under different references</strong>:</p>
169	
170	<table>
171	  <tr><th>Input</th><th>Reference (VR+)</th><th>Code (12-bit)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
172	  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>1.2 V (VREF)</td><td><code>4095</code></td><td>Full scale — input equals the reference</td></tr>
173	  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>2.0 V (VREF2.0)</td><td><code>2457</code></td><td>60% of span</td></tr>
174	  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>2.5 V (VREF2.5)</td><td><code>1965</code></td><td>48% of span</td></tr>
175	  <tr><td>1.2 V</td><td>3.3 V (AVCC)</td><td><code>1489</code></td><td>≈36% of span</td></tr>
176	</table>
177	
178	<div class="callout tip">
179	  <div class="callout-label">💡 The mental model</div>
180	  Think of the reference as a ruler. The ADC reports "the input reaches the 1489th tick mark of my 4096-tick ruler." If you swap in a shorter ruler (a smaller reference), the same input reaches a <em>higher</em> tick mark. The tick number is meaningless until you know which ruler was used.
181	</div>
182	</section>
183	
184	<!-- ============================================================ -->
185	<section>
186	<h2>2. The Transfer Function — SLAU367P §34.2.1, Equation 13</h2>
187	
188	<p>TI's MSP430FR58xx/59xx family user's guide (SLAU367P) defines the ADC12_B conversion result in §34.2.1, Equation 13:</p>
189	
190	<div class="equation">
191	  N<sub>ADC</sub> &nbsp;=&nbsp; <span class="fs">4095</span> &nbsp;×&nbsp;
192	  <span class="frac">
193	    <span class="top"><span class="vin">V<sub>in</sub></span> − <span class="vr">V<sub>R−</sub></span></span>
194	    <span class="bot"><span class="vr">V<sub>R+</sub></span> − <span class="vr">V<sub>R−</sub></span></span>
195	  </span>
196	  <span class="eq-note">4095 = full-scale code for 12-bit mode; more generally FS = 2<sup>resolution</sup> − 1</span>
197	</div>
198	
199	<ul>
200	  <li><strong>V<sub>in</sub></strong> — the analog input voltage on the selected channel.</li>
201	  <li><strong>V<sub>R+</sub></strong> — the positive reference: the voltage that maps to full scale. Selected by <code>ADC12VRSEL</code>.</li>
202	  <li><strong>V<sub>R−</sub></strong> — the negative reference: the voltage that maps to code 0. In Silmulator's model it is fixed at AVSS (0 V), so the equation simplifies to <code>N = FS × Vin / VR+</code>.</li>
203	  <li><strong>FS</strong> — the full-scale code: 4095 (12-bit), 1023 (10-bit), or 255 (8-bit), matching <code>ADC12RES</code>.</li>
204	</ul>
205	
206	<h3>Worked example 1 — the probe case: 1.2 V input against the 1.2-V VREF</h3>
207	<pre><code>N = 4095 × (1.2 − 0) / (1.2 − 0)
208	  = 4095 × 1.0
209	  = <span class="num">4095</span>   <span class="cm"># full scale — the input IS the reference</span></code></pre>
210	
211	<h3>Worked example 2 — the same 1.2 V input against AVCC = 3.3 V</h3>
212	<pre><code>N = 4095 × (1.2 − 0) / (3.3 − 0)
213	  = 4095 × 0.36363…
214	  = 1489.09  →  int()  →  <span class="num">1489</span></code></pre>
215	
216	<p>Identical input, two references, codes of <strong>4095 vs 1489</strong>. This exact pair is the audit probe (<code>adc_ignores_reference</code>) that exposed the bug: the old model returned 1489 <em>regardless</em> of the selected reference.</p>
217	
218	<h3>Worked example 3 — recovering volts from a code</h3>
219	<p>Firmware and test scripts invert the transfer function to get volts back:</p>
220	<pre><code>V<sub>in</sub> ≈ N / FS × (V<sub>R+</sub> − V<sub>R−</sub>) + V<sub>R−</sub>
221	     = 1489 / 4095 × 3.3
222	     = <span class="num">1.19993 V</span>   <span class="cm"># ~1.2 V recovered — but ONLY if you divide by the SAME reference</span></code></pre>
223	
224	<div class="callout warn">
225	  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Truncation, not rounding</div>
226	  Silmulator's nominal profile uses <code>int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code)</code> — Python's <code>int()</code> truncates toward zero. An ideal result of 2047.5 becomes <code>2047</code>, not 2048. Keep this in mind when predicting exact codes in the drill.
227	</div>
228	</section>
229	
230	<!-- ============================================================ -->
231	<section>
232	<h2>3. The Fix — What Commit 58b7e2d Actually Changed</h2>
233	
234	<p>Issue #18 came out of the MSP430 compliance audit (<code>docs/audits/msp430-compliance-report.md</code>). Two ADC families were NON_COMPLIANT for the same root cause: the model ignored the reference.</p>
235	
236	<h3>Before: a hardcoded 3.3 in the conversion path</h3>
237	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/silmulator/devices/msp430.py — BEFORE 58b7e2d</span>
238	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_adc_ch_raw</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ac: <span class="cls">ADCChannel</span>) -> str:
239	    <span class="del">return str(self.faults.adc_code(ac.value, 3.3, ac.resolution))</span>
240	                                              <span class="cm"># ↑ VR+ frozen at 3.3 V forever</span></code></pre>
241	
242	<p>And <code>ADC:REFERENCE</code> stored <em>any</em> string without complaint — <code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code> succeeded, and the stored value was never read by the conversion code. Two independent defects: <strong>no validation</strong> and <strong>no effect</strong>.</p>
243	
244	<h3>After: VR+ is resolved from the selected reference at conversion time</h3>
245	<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/silmulator/devices/msp430.py — AFTER 58b7e2d</span>
246	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_adc_ch_raw</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ac: <span class="cls">ADCChannel</span>) -> str:
247	    <span class="add">return str(self.faults.adc_code(ac.value, self._adc_reference_voltage(), ac.resolution))</span>
248	
249	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_adc_reference_voltage</span>(self) -> float:
250	    <span class="str">"""VR+ in volts for the selected ADC reference (SLAU367P §34.2.1 Eq. 13).
251	
252	    VR- is AVSS (0 V) in this model — the simulator does not expose the
253	    VR- half of ADC12VRSEL's combinations, only which source drives VR+.
254	    """</span>
255	    ref = self.adc[<span class="num">0</span>].reference
256	    <span class="kw">if</span> ref == <span class="str">"AVCC"</span>:
257	        <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.vcc          <span class="cm"># reads CPU:VCC live — not a snapshot</span>
258	    <span class="kw">if</span> ref == <span class="str">"VEREF"</span>:
259	        <span class="kw">return</span> self._veref_v           <span class="cm"># the external VeREF+ pin voltage</span>
260	    <span class="kw">return</span> ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V.get(ref, self.clock.vcc)  <span class="cm"># REF_A outputs</span></code></pre>
261	
262	<h3>The new module-level reference table</h3>
263	<pre><code><span class="cm"># REF_A's three internal reference outputs (SLAU367P §33.3.1, REFVSEL field).</span>
264	<span class="cm"># Bare "VREF" maps to REFVSEL's reset value (00b = 1.2 V).</span>
265	ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V: <span class="cls">Dict</span>[str, float] = {
266	    <span class="str">"VREF"</span>:    <span class="num">1.2</span>,
267	    <span class="str">"VREF1.2"</span>: <span class="num">1.2</span>,
268	    <span class="str">"VREF2.0"</span>: <span class="num">2.0</span>,
269	    <span class="str">"VREF2.5"</span>: <span class="num">2.5</span>,
270	}
271	
272	<span class="cm"># The fixed set ADC:REFERENCE accepts: AVCC (tracks CPU:VCC), REF_A's internal</span>
273	<span class="cm"># outputs, and VEREF (the external VeREF+ pin). Anything else has no</span>
274	<span class="cm"># ADC12VRSEL encoding and is rejected with -224.</span>
275	VALID_ADC_REFERENCES = <span class="fn">frozenset</span>({<span class="str">"AVCC"</span>, <span class="str">"VEREF"</span>} | <span class="fn">set</span>(ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V))</code></pre>
276	
277	<h3>Validation in the SCPI handler</h3>
278	<pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_adc_ref</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> str:
279	    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query:
280	        <span class="kw">return</span> self.adc[<span class="num">0</span>].reference
281	    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:
282	        ref = cmd.params[<span class="num">0</span>].upper()
283	        <span class="kw">if</span> ref <span class="kw">not in</span> VALID_ADC_REFERENCES:
284	            <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">224</span>, f<span class="str">"Illegal ADC reference: {ref}"</span>)
285	        <span class="kw">if</span> ref == <span class="str">"VEREF"</span> <span class="kw">and</span> len(cmd.params) > <span class="num">1</span>:
286	            <span class="kw">try</span>:
287	                self._veref_v = <span class="fn">float</span>(cmd.params[<span class="num">1</span>])
288	            <span class="kw">except</span> <span class="cls">ValueError</span>:
289	                <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">104</span>, f<span class="str">"Invalid VEREF voltage: {cmd.params[1]}"</span>)
290	        <span class="kw">for</span> ch <span class="kw">in</span> self.adc.values():
291	            ch.reference = ref     <span class="cm"># one reference for all channels (single-VR+ model)</span>
292	    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span></code></pre>
293	
294	<h3>Tests flipped from expected-failure to passing</h3>
295	<p>The two audit-derived tests dropped their <code>@pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True)</code> decorators — the code now does what SLAU367P says:</p>
296	<pre><code><span class="cm"># tests/test_msp430_edge_cases.py — AFTER 58b7e2d (xfail decorators removed)</span>
297	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_reference_out_of_range_rejected</span>(self, mcu):
298	    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §34.2.4 (ADC12VRSEL), §33 (REF_A): the reference is selected</span>
299	    <span class="cm"># from a fixed set (AVCC, 1.2/2.0/2.5-V REF, external) — not free-form.</span>
300	    mcu.process(<span class="str">"ADC:REFERENCE BANANA"</span>)
301	    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">errors</span>(mcu) == <span class="num">1</span>
302	
303	<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_raw_respects_selected_reference</span>(self, mcu):
304	    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §34.2.1 Eq. 13: with VR+ = 1.2-V VREF, a 1.2-V input is full scale.</span>
305	    mcu.process(<span class="str">"ADC:REFERENCE VREF"</span>)
306	    mcu.inject_adc(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1.2</span>)
307	    <span class="kw">assert</span> int(mcu.process(<span class="str">"ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?"</span>)) == <span class="num">4095</span></code></pre>
308	
309	<p>Compliance moved from <strong>19.8% → 22.8%</strong>: <code>ADC:CHANNELn:RAW</code>, <code>ADC:CHn:RAW</code>, and <code>ADC:REFERENCE</code> all flipped NON_COMPLIANT → COMPLIANT. (<code>ADC:CONVERT</code> now scales correctly too but stays NON_COMPLIANT for unrelated reasons: it runs without <code>ADC12ENC</code> and returns <code>'0'</code> for nonexistent channels.)</p>
310	
311	<div class="callout">
312	  <div class="callout-label">📌 One more detail: *RST</div>
313	  The new VeREF+ pin state <code>self._veref_v</code> is reset to 1.2 in the <code>*RST</code> handler, alongside all other peripheral state. Any new device state you add must be reset there too — that's the *RST contract.
314	</div>
315	</section>
