317	<!-- ============================================================ -->
318	<section>
319	<h2>4. Why References Are a Fixed Hardware Set (and -224 for Everything Else)</h2>
320	
321	<p>On a real MSP430FR5994 you cannot ask for a "1.8 V reference". The positive reference is selected by <strong>ADC12VRSEL</strong> — a 4-bit field in <code>ADC12MCTLx</code> (SLAU367P §34.2.4) whose encodings pick which <em>physical voltage source</em> drives VR+ and VR−. A register field with a finite set of encodings is the hardware truth the simulator must mirror.</p>
322	
323	<h3>The sources Silmulator models</h3>
324	<table>
325	  <tr><th>SCPI token</th><th>Physical source</th><th>VR+ value</th></tr>
326	  <tr><td><code>AVCC</code></td><td>The analog supply rail</td><td>Whatever <code>CPU:VCC</code> is — read <em>live</em> at conversion time (default 3.3 V)</td></tr>
327	  <tr><td><code>VREF</code></td><td>REF_A internal reference, REFVSEL reset default (00b)</td><td>1.2 V</td></tr>
328	  <tr><td><code>VREF1.2</code></td><td>REF_A internal reference, REFVSEL = 00b</td><td>1.2 V</td></tr>
329	  <tr><td><code>VREF2.0</code></td><td>REF_A internal reference, REFVSEL = 01b</td><td>2.0 V</td></tr>
330	  <tr><td><code>VREF2.5</code></td><td>REF_A internal reference, REFVSEL = 10b</td><td>2.5 V</td></tr>
331	  <tr><td><code>VEREF</code></td><td>The external VeREF+ pin (board-supplied)</td><td>Whatever the test sets, e.g. <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1</code> (default 1.2 V)</td></tr>
332	</table>
333	
334	<h3>Three different kinds of reference source</h3>
335	<ul>
336	  <li><strong>AVCC</strong> — the supply itself. Cheap and always available, but it moves with the supply: brown-outs, battery droop, and regulator tolerance all show up as measurement error. Silmulator models this honestly: <code>_adc_reference_voltage()</code> reads <code>self.clock.vcc</code> at every conversion, so <code>CPU:VCC 3.0</code> immediately rescales all subsequent conversions.</li>
337	  <li><strong>REF_A internal outputs</strong> — the FR5994's shared reference module (SLAU367P §33) generates precise 1.2 / 2.0 / 2.5 V from an internal bandgap, independent of the supply. This is what you use when the measurement must be in absolute volts.</li>
338	  <li><strong>VeREF+</strong> — an external pin (SLASE54D Table 7-1) for boards that provide their own precision reference IC. Its voltage is whatever the board designer wired up — which is why Silmulator lets the test declare it: <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.048</code>.</li>
339	</ul>
340	
341	<h3>Why -224 specifically?</h3>
342	<p>SCPI error <strong>-224, "Illegal parameter value"</strong> is the standard code for "the parameter is the right type but not one of the permitted values." The token <code>BANANA</code> (or <code>1.8</code>, or <code>3V3</code>) parses fine as a string — it just has <em>no ADC12VRSEL encoding</em>. Compare:</p>
343	<ul>
344	  <li><code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code> → <strong>-224</strong> — not in the value set. The stored reference is left unchanged (still the <code>AVCC</code> default).</li>
345	  <li><code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,notanumber</code> → <strong>-104, "Data type error"</strong> — the reference token is valid, but the second parameter must be numeric.</li>
346	  <li><code>ADC:RESOLUTION 9</code> → <strong>-224</strong> — the sibling precedent: <code>ADC12RES</code> only encodes 8/10/12 bits, so 9 is rejected the same way.</li>
347	</ul>
348	
349	<div class="callout tip">
350	  <div class="callout-label">💡 The design rule</div>
351	  When a SCPI parameter mirrors a hardware register field, the accepted value set is exactly the register's encodings — no more, no less. Free-form acceptance is how a simulator ends up blessing configurations that no silicon can express.
352	</div>
353	</section>
354	
355	<!-- ============================================================ -->
356	<section>
357	<h2>5. Full Scale, Clipping, and the LSB</h2>
358	
359	<h3>Clipping: inputs above VR+ saturate</h3>
360	<p><code>faults.adc_code()</code> clamps the result to <code>[0, max_code]</code>. An input above the reference doesn't wrap or error — it pegs at full scale, exactly like real hardware:</p>
361	<pre><code><span class="cm"># msp430_faults.py — nominal profile</span>
362	max_code = (<span class="num">1</span> &lt;&lt; resolution) - <span class="num">1</span>            <span class="cm"># 4095 / 1023 / 255</span>
363	code = int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code)
364	<span class="kw">return</span> max(<span class="num">0</span>, min(max_code, code))          <span class="cm"># ← the clamp</span></code></pre>
365	<pre><code>Inject 2.5 V, reference VREF2.0:
366	N = 4095 × 2.5 / 2.0 = 5118.75  →  clamped  →  <span class="num">4095</span></code></pre>
367	<p>Reading 4095 therefore means "<em>at or above</em> the reference" — the true voltage is unrecoverable. A real design leaves headroom; a SIL test can now verify that firmware treats 4095 as saturation, not as a valid measurement.</p>
368	
369	<h3>The LSB: what one code step is worth</h3>
370	<p>One least-significant-bit step corresponds to <code>(VR+ − VR−) / FS</code> volts. The reference sets not just the range but the <strong>granularity</strong>:</p>
371	<table>
372	  <tr><th>Reference</th><th>Range</th><th>1 LSB (12-bit)</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
373	  <tr><td>VREF 1.2 V</td><td>0 – 1.2 V</td><td>≈ 293 µV</td><td>Finest resolution, smallest range</td></tr>
374	  <tr><td>VREF2.0</td><td>0 – 2.0 V</td><td>≈ 488 µV</td><td></td></tr>
375	  <tr><td>VREF2.5</td><td>0 – 2.5 V</td><td>≈ 611 µV</td><td></td></tr>
376	  <tr><td>AVCC 3.3 V</td><td>0 – 3.3 V</td><td>≈ 806 µV</td><td>Widest range, coarsest steps, supply-dependent</td></tr>
377	</table>
378	<p>This is why real firmware picks the smallest reference that still covers the signal: a 0–1 V sensor read against the 1.2-V VREF uses ~83% of the code space; against AVCC it uses only ~30%, throwing away 1.4 bits of effective resolution.</p>
379	
380	<h3>Resolution interacts the same way</h3>
381	<p><code>ADC:RESOLUTION 8</code> changes FS to 255: injecting 3.3 V against AVCC 3.3 gives <code>int(3.3/3.3 × 255) = 255</code> — the existing edge-case test. Reference and resolution are the two knobs of the same equation.</p>
382	
383	<h3>Ratiometric measurement: when AVCC is the right answer</h3>
384	<p>If the sensor itself is powered from AVCC (a potentiometer, a resistive bridge), its output is already a <em>fraction of AVCC</em>. Converting it against AVCC makes the supply cancel out of the equation:</p>
385	<div class="equation">
386	  N = FS × <span class="frac"><span class="top">k · <span class="vr">AVCC</span></span><span class="bot"><span class="vr">AVCC</span></span></span> = FS × k
387	  <span class="eq-note">the code depends only on the sensor's fraction k — supply drift disappears</span>
388	</div>
389	<p>This is <strong>ratiometric measurement</strong>, and it is the reason "AVCC" exists as a reference choice at all. Silmulator's live <code>clock.vcc</code> read makes this testable: drop <code>CPU:VCC</code> and a ratiometric setup's code stays put, while an absolute-reference setup's code shifts.</p>
390	</section>
391	
392	<!-- ============================================================ -->
393	<section>
394	<h2>6. Why This Matters for SIL Testing</h2>
395	
396	<p>Silmulator's purpose is to catch embedded-firmware bugs <em>before</em> hardware exists. The hardcoded 3.3 V broke that promise in a very specific way — it made the simulator <strong>agree with buggy firmware</strong>.</p>
397	
398	<h3>The bug class that was invisible</h3>
399	<p>Consider firmware that configures the 2.5-V internal reference but converts codes to volts with the wrong constant:</p>
400	<pre><code><span class="cm">/* firmware under test — BUG: reference is 2.5 V, math assumes 3.3 V */</span>
401	<span class="kw">float</span> <span class="fn">read_battery_volts</span>(<span class="kw">void</span>) {
402	    <span class="fn">select_reference</span>(REF_2V5);            <span class="cm">/* ADC12VRSEL = internal 2.5 V   */</span>
403	    uint16_t raw = <span class="fn">adc_convert</span>(CH0);
404	    <span class="kw">return</span> raw / <span class="num">4095.0f</span> * <span class="num">3.3f</span>;          <span class="cm">/* ← wrong unit of measure!      */</span>
405	}</code></pre>
406	<ul>
407	  <li><strong>Old model:</strong> the simulated ADC also divided by 3.3, so <code>raw/4095×3.3</code> round-tripped perfectly. The test passed. The bug shipped.</li>
408	  <li><strong>New model:</strong> injecting 1.5 V gives <code>raw = int(1.5/2.5×4095) = 2457</code>; the firmware computes <code>2457/4095×3.3 = 1.98 V</code>. The SIL test's assertion <code>≈1.5 V</code> fails — the mis-scaling is caught in simulation, exactly where it should be.</li>
409	</ul>
410	
411	<h3>What else becomes testable</h3>
412	<ul>
413	  <li><strong>Reference-selection typos:</strong> firmware SCPI-driving a bad token now queues -224 instead of silently "working".</li>
414	  <li><strong>Supply-droop behaviour:</strong> <code>CPU:VCC 3.0</code> rescales AVCC-referenced conversions live (1.2 V now reads <code>1638</code> instead of <code>1489</code>) — brown-out compensation logic can be exercised.</li>
415	  <li><strong>Saturation handling:</strong> over-range inputs peg at 4095; firmware that treats 4095 as a real reading can be flagged.</li>
416	  <li><strong>Board-specific references:</strong> <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.048</code> models a precision external reference IC without any Python changes.</li>
417	</ul>
418	
419	<div class="callout warn">
420	  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The general lesson</div>
421	  A simulator defect is worse than a missing feature. A missing feature fails loudly; a wrong constant makes the simulator and the buggy firmware wrong <em>in the same direction</em>, and every test between them passes. That is why the compliance audit probes behaviour (<code>adc_ignores_reference</code>) rather than trusting the command to exist.
422	</div>
423	</section>
