530	<section>
531	<h2>9. Scenarios — Check Your Understanding</h2>
532	
533	<div class="scenario">
534	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario A: Your board has a precision 2.048-V reference IC wired to the VeREF+ pin. Write the SCPI sequence to configure the simulated MSP430 to match, inject 1.024 V on channel 2, and predict the 12-bit RAW result. What error would you get if you typo'd the voltage as <code>2.O48</code> (letter O)?</div>
535	  <details>
536	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
537	    <div class="ans">
538	      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.048     ← selects the external pin AND sets its voltage
539	ADC:CHANNEL2:INJECT 1.024
540	ADC:CHANNEL2:RAW?             → int(1.024 / 2.048 × 4095) = int(2047.5) = 2047</pre>
541	      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">Exactly half the reference → half of full scale (truncated from 2047.5). With <code>ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.O48</code>, the token <code>VEREF</code> is valid but <code>float("2.O48")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>, so the handler queues <strong>-104, "Invalid VEREF voltage: 2.O48"</strong> — a data-type error, not -224. The previously stored <code>_veref_v</code> is untouched. Also remember <code>*RST</code> resets <code>_veref_v</code> to 1.2, so the sequence must run after any reset.</p>
542	    </div>
543	  </details>
544	</div>
545	
546	<div class="scenario">
547	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario B: A teammate reports "the ADC model is broken — I inject 1.2 V and RAW? returns 4095, but 1.2/3.3×4095 should be 1489." What do you check first, and what do you tell them?</div>
548	  <details>
549	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
550	    <div class="ans">
551	      <p>Check the selected reference: <code>ADC:REFERENCE?</code>. Almost certainly it returns <code>VREF</code> — someone (or a previous test, since the reference persists until *RST) selected the internal 1.2-V reference. Under VR+ = 1.2 V, a 1.2-V input <em>is</em> full scale: N = 4095 × 1.2/1.2 = 4095. This is the exact behaviour <code>test_raw_respects_selected_reference</code> asserts.</p>
552	      <p>The teammate's formula smuggles in the assumption that 3.3 V is the unit of measure. The 1489 figure is only correct when AVCC (at its 3.3-V default) is selected. Their expectation is the pre-58b7e2d bug — the model used to behave that way, and it was NON_COMPLIANT. The fix: state expected codes as a function of the selected reference, or explicitly select <code>ADC:REFERENCE AVCC</code> at test setup.</p>
553	    </div>
554	  </details>
555	</div>
556	
557	<div class="scenario">
558	  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario C: You're writing a SIL test for battery-monitoring firmware that must keep working as the supply sags from 3.3 V to 3.0 V. The firmware measures a fixed 1.2-V bandgap-derived signal using AVCC as the reference and infers the supply from the code. Using only SCPI, how do you simulate the sag and what codes should the test expect at each supply level?</div>
559	  <details>
560	    <summary>Show answer</summary>
561	    <div class="ans">
562	      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">ADC:REFERENCE AVCC            ← unit of measure = the live supply
563	ADC:CHANNEL0:INJECT 1.2       ← the fixed signal being measured
564	
565	CPU:VCC 3.3
566	ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?             → int(1.2/3.3 × 4095) = 1489
567	
568	CPU:VCC 3.0                   ← simulate the sag
569	ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?             → int(1.2/3.0 × 4095) = 1638</pre>
570	      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">Because <code>_adc_reference_voltage()</code> reads <code>clock.vcc</code> live, no re-arming is needed — the very next conversion uses the new supply. The trick the firmware exploits is the transfer function in reverse: the <em>input</em> is fixed, so a rising code means a falling reference. AVCC ≈ 4095 × 1.2 / N, so N = 1638 → AVCC ≈ 3.0 V. This measure-the-ruler-with-a-known-stick technique is only testable because the model stopped hardcoding 3.3 V.</p>
571	    </div>
572	  </details>
573	</div>
