220	// ─── CHANNEL: transfer ────────────────────────────────────────
221	{id:'t1',ch:'transfer',type:'w',
222	 q:'Write out the ADC12_B transfer function from SLAU367P §34.2.1 (Eq. 13) and explain what each of the four quantities means. What does the equation simplify to in Silmulator\'s model, and why?',
223	 c:[{l:'NADC = FS × (Vin − VR−)/(VR+ − VR−)',t:['4095','fs ×','(vin','vr−','vr-','vr+']},
224	    {l:'VR+ = positive reference (full-scale voltage)',t:['vr+','positive reference','full scale','full-scale']},
225	    {l:'VR− = negative reference (code-0 voltage)',t:['vr−','vr-','negative reference','code 0','zero']},
226	    {l:'VR− fixed at AVSS (0 V) → N = FS × Vin/VR+',t:['avss','0 v','fixed','simplif','vin / vr','vin/vr']}],
227	 m:`NADC = FS × (Vin − VR−) / (VR+ − VR−), where FS = 2^resolution − 1 (4095 in 12-bit mode).
228	- Vin: the analog input voltage on the selected channel.
229	- VR+: the positive reference — the voltage that produces the full-scale code. Selected by ADC12VRSEL.
230	- VR−: the negative reference — the voltage that produces code 0.
231	- FS: the full-scale code (4095 / 1023 / 255 for 12/10/8-bit ADC12RES modes).
232	In Silmulator, VR− is fixed at AVSS (0 V) — the _adc_reference_voltage() docstring says the model only exposes which source drives VR+, not the VR− half of ADC12VRSEL's combinations. So the equation simplifies to N = FS × Vin / VR+, implemented as int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code), clamped to [0, max_code].`
233	},
234	
235	{id:'t2',ch:'transfer',type:'mc',
236	 q:'ADC:REFERENCE AVCC is selected (CPU:VCC at its 3.3-V default) and 1.2 V is injected on channel 0. What does ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? return in 12-bit mode?',
237	 snip:`<span class="cm"># msp430_faults.py — nominal profile</span>
238	code = int(voltage / full_scale_v * max_code)
239	<span class="kw">return</span> max(<span class="num">0</span>, min(max_code, code))`,
240	 opts:[
241	  {t:'1489',c:true, w:'Correct. int(1.2 / 3.3 × 4095) = int(1489.09) = 1489 — about 36% of the reference span.'},
242	  {t:'4095',c:false,w:'4095 would require Vin ≥ VR+. That happens with the same 1.2-V input only when the 1.2-V VREF is the selected reference.'},
243	  {t:'1490',c:false,w:'int() truncates: 1489.09 → 1489. There is no rounding to nearest in adc_code().'},
244	  {t:'1229',c:false,w:'1229 ≈ int(1.2/4.0×4095) — wrong reference voltage. AVCC defaults to 3.3 V (clock.vcc).'},
245	 ]
246	},
247	
248	{id:'t3',ch:'transfer',type:'mc',
249	 q:'Same 1.2-V injection on channel 0, but now the test first sends ADC:REFERENCE VREF. What does ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? return?',
250	 opts:[
251	  {t:'4095 — the input equals the 1.2-V reference, so it reads full scale',c:true, w:'Correct. Bare VREF maps to REFVSEL\'s reset default (00b = 1.2 V) via ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V. N = 4095 × 1.2/1.2 = 4095. This is exactly test_raw_respects_selected_reference and the adc_ignores_reference audit probe.'},
252	  {t:'1489 — the reference selection is stored but conversions always use 3.3 V',c:false,w:'That was the pre-58b7e2d bug. The fix makes _adc_ch_raw call _adc_reference_voltage(), so VREF (1.2 V) is actually used.'},
253	  {t:'2047 — VREF means half of AVCC',c:false,w:'VREF is REF_A\'s internal 1.2-V output (REFVSEL reset value), not a fraction of the supply.'},
254	  {t:'SCPI error -224 — bare VREF is ambiguous and must be VREF1.2/2.0/2.5',c:false,w:'Bare VREF is valid: it maps to the REFVSEL reset default of 1.2 V in ADC_INTERNAL_REF_V.'},
255	 ]
256	},
257	
258	{id:'t4',ch:'transfer',type:'w',
259	 q:'A 0.6-V signal is injected on channel 5. Compute the 12-bit RAW code under each of these three references: VREF1.2, VREF2.5, and AVCC (3.3 V). Show your arithmetic, including the truncation step.',
260	 c:[{l:'VREF1.2 → 2047 (0.6/1.2×4095 = 2047.5, truncated)',t:['2047']},
261	    {l:'VREF2.5 → 982 (0.6/2.5×4095 = 982.8)',t:['982']},
262	    {l:'AVCC 3.3 → 744 (0.6/3.3×4095 = 744.5)',t:['744']},
263	    {l:'int() truncates toward zero',t:['truncat','int(','toward zero','floor']}],
264	 m:`All use N = int(0.6 / VR+ × 4095):
265	- VREF1.2: 0.6/1.2 = 0.5 → 0.5 × 4095 = 2047.5 → int() truncates → 2047
266	- VREF2.5: 0.6/2.5 = 0.24 → 0.24 × 4095 = 982.8 → 982
267	- AVCC 3.3: 0.6/3.3 ≈ 0.1818 → × 4095 = 744.54 → 744
268	Same physical voltage, three different codes — the code is a fraction of the selected reference, not a voltage. Note int() truncates toward zero (2047.5 → 2047, never 2048).`
269	},
