311	{id:'r3',ch:'refs',type:'mc',
312	 q:'Why does the simulator reject ADC:REFERENCE 1.8 rather than supporting arbitrary reference voltages?',
313	 opts:[
314	  {t:'The real ADC12VRSEL register field has a finite set of encodings — there is no silicon path that produces an arbitrary 1.8-V reference',c:true, w:'Correct. VR+ must come from a physical source: AVCC, one of REF_A\'s three bandgap outputs, or the external VeREF+ pin. A free-form voltage would let SIL tests bless configurations no hardware can express — the simulator must mirror the register\'s value set.'},
315	  {t:'Floating-point parameters are not supported by the SCPI parser',c:false,w:'The parser handles floats fine — ADC:REFERENCE VEREF,2.1 takes one. The rejection is about hardware fidelity, not parsing.'},
316	  {t:'1.8 V would exceed the maximum safe reference for the FR5994',c:false,w:'2.5-V and 3.3-V references are valid, so 1.8 V is not out of range. It simply corresponds to no ADC12VRSEL encoding.'},
317	  {t:'It is a temporary limitation until REF_A trimming is modelled',c:false,w:'It is deliberate and permanent: the accepted set mirrors the hardware\'s selectable sources. (What IS still missing from REF_A is REFCTL0 semantics — REFON/REFOUT bits etc.)'},
318	 ]
