333	{id:'r5',ch:'refs',type:'mc',
334	 q:'What is the difference between SCPI errors -224 and -104 in the ADC:REFERENCE handler?',
335	 opts:[
336	  {t:'-224 = token not in the permitted value set (e.g. BANANA); -104 = right token but the VEREF voltage parameter fails float() parsing',c:true, w:'Correct. -224 "Illegal parameter value": correct type, not an allowed value. -104 "Data type error": the second parameter must be numeric. Two different failure modes, two standard SCPI codes.'},
337	  {t:'-224 is fatal and halts the device; -104 is a warning',c:false,w:'Neither halts anything — both queue an error and leave device state unchanged. Severity is identical; the meaning differs.'},
338	  {t:'They are interchangeable; the codes were chosen arbitrarily',c:false,w:'They follow the SCPI standard\'s error classification: -1xx = command/data-type errors, -2xx = execution errors like illegal parameter values.'},
339	  {t:'-104 is used for unknown reference tokens, -224 for bad voltages',c:false,w:'It is the reverse: -224 for the unknown token (BANANA), -104 for the malformed voltage (VEREF,notanumber).'},
340	 ]
