397	 q:'Two tests in test_msp430_edge_cases.py had @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) removed by commit 58b7e2d. Name them, state what each asserts, and explain why strict xfail was the right marker before the fix.',
398	 c:[{l:'test_reference_out_of_range_rejected — BANANA queues 1 error',t:['out_of_range','banana','rejected','errors(mcu) == 1','one error']},
399	    {l:'test_raw_respects_selected_reference — VREF + 1.2 V → 4095',t:['respects_selected','4095','vref','1.2']},
400	    {l:'strict xfail = documented known bug; XPASS fails the suite when fixed',t:['strict','xpass','known bug','documented','flip','fails if it passes']}],
401	 m:`1. test_reference_out_of_range_rejected — sends ADC:REFERENCE BANANA and asserts errors(mcu) == 1, citing SLAU367P §34.2.4/§33: the reference comes from a fixed set, not free-form.
402	2. test_raw_respects_selected_reference — selects VREF, injects 1.2 V, asserts ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW? == 4095 (Eq. 13: input equal to the reference is full scale).
403	Before the fix, both were expected failures documenting audit findings. strict=True is the key: a strict xfail FAILS the suite if the test unexpectedly passes (XPASS). That means the test file doubles as a ledger of known non-compliances — the moment someone fixes the behaviour, the strict xfail forces them to remove the marker and promote the test to a real regression guard, which is exactly what 58b7e2d did. The commit also ADDED test_veref_voltage_param_must_be_numeric for the new -104 path.`
