427	{id:'k2',ch:'clip',type:'w',
428	 q:'Define "LSB voltage" via the transfer function, compute it for VREF1.2 and AVCC 3.3 in 12-bit mode, and state the engineering trade-off in choosing a smaller reference.',
429	 c:[{l:'LSB = (VR+ − VR−)/FS — the voltage of one code step',t:['(vr+','/ 4095','/4095','one code','step','fs']},
430	    {l:'1.2 V → ≈293 µV/LSB',t:['293']},
431	    {l:'3.3 V → ≈806 µV/LSB',t:['806','805']},
432	    {l:'smaller ref = finer resolution but smaller range (clipping above VR+)',t:['finer','smaller range','clip','trade','resolution','headroom']}],
433	 m:`One LSB is the input voltage change that moves the code by one step: LSB = (VR+ − VR−) / FS = VR+ / 4095 in this model.
434	- VREF1.2: 1.2 / 4095 ≈ 293 µV per code.
435	- AVCC 3.3: 3.3 / 4095 ≈ 806 µV per code.
436	Trade-off: a smaller reference gives finer granularity (each code is worth fewer microvolts) but shrinks the measurable range — anything above VR+ clips to 4095. So you pick the smallest reference that still covers the signal with headroom. A 0–1 V signal against VREF1.2 uses ~83% of the code space; against AVCC it uses ~30%, wasting ~1.4 bits of effective resolution.`
437	},
