449	{id:'k4',ch:'clip',type:'w',
450	 q:'A pressure sensor is a resistive divider powered from AVCC, outputting a fixed fraction k of the supply. Explain why converting it against AVCC (ratiometric measurement) is MORE accurate than using the precise VREF2.5 when the supply drifts — derive it from Eq. 13.',
451	 c:[{l:'sensor output = k × AVCC',t:['k × avcc','k×avcc','fraction of the supply','fraction of avcc','k * avcc']},
452	    {l:'against AVCC: N = FS × k·AVCC/AVCC = FS×k — supply cancels',t:['cancel','fs × k','fs×k','independent of','drops out']},
453	    {l:'against VREF2.5: N = FS × k·AVCC/2.5 — code moves with supply drift',t:['2.5','moves','drift','tracks the supply','changes with']},
454	    {l:'testable in Silmulator via live clock.vcc / CPU:VCC',t:['cpu:vcc','clock.vcc','live','silmulator','simulat']}],
455	 m:`The sensor's output is Vin = k × AVCC (a divider scales its own supply).
456	Against AVCC as reference: N = FS × (k × AVCC) / AVCC = FS × k. AVCC cancels — the code measures the sensor fraction k directly and is immune to supply drift. That is ratiometric measurement, and it is why AVCC exists as a reference choice.
457	Against VREF2.5: N = FS × (k × AVCC) / 2.5. Now AVCC stays in the equation, so a supply sag from 3.3 to 3.0 V changes the code by ~9% even though the pressure (k) is unchanged — the "precise" absolute reference is the wrong tool for a ratiometric sensor.
458	Silmulator can now exercise this because _adc_reference_voltage() reads clock.vcc live: set CPU:VCC 3.0 and an AVCC-referenced divider reading (if you re-inject k × 3.0) holds its code, while a VREF2.5-referenced one shifts.`
459	},
