462	{id:'s1',ch:'sil',type:'w',
463	 q:'Explain the "cancelling errors" failure mode: why did the pre-58b7e2d simulator make SIL tests PASS on firmware that decoded ADC codes with the wrong reference constant?',
464	 c:[{l:'sim encoded with hardcoded 3.3 regardless of selection',t:['3.3','hardcoded','encode','ignored the reference','regardless']},
465	    {l:'buggy firmware decoded with the same 3.3',t:['decode','raw/4095','firmware','same constant','×3.3','* 3.3']},
466	    {l:'errors cancelled → round-trip looked correct → test passed',t:['cancel','round-trip','round trip','passed','same direction','agree']},
467	    {l:'fix breaks the cancellation → mis-scaling now caught',t:['fix','caught','fails','breaks','now fail','expose']}],
468	 m:`The round-trip was: simulator encodes N = int(Vin/3.3 × 4095); firmware decodes V = N/4095 × 3.3. If firmware wrongly assumed 3.3 V while having selected the 2.5-V reference, the simulator — which ALSO ignored the selection and used 3.3 — encoded with the same wrong constant. The two errors cancelled exactly: V came back ≈ Vin, assertions passed, and the reference-scaling bug shipped to hardware where the real ADC encodes with 2.5 V.
469	A simulator defect that agrees with a firmware defect is worse than a missing feature: it actively certifies broken code. After 58b7e2d the simulator encodes with the SELECTED reference (2.5), the firmware decodes with 3.3, and the recovered voltage is off by 3.3/2.5 = 1.32× — the SIL assertion fails, catching the bug in simulation where it belongs.`
470	},
