487	{id:'s3',ch:'sil',type:'w',
488	 q:'Write the SCPI sequence for a SIL test that verifies brown-out rescaling: prove that AVCC-referenced conversions track a supply sag from 3.3 V to 3.0 V with a fixed 1.2-V input. Give the expected codes.',
489	 c:[{l:'ADC:REFERENCE AVCC + inject 1.2',t:['reference avcc','inject 1.2','inject']},
490	    {l:'at VCC 3.3 → 1489',t:['1489']},
491	    {l:'CPU:VCC 3.0 then re-read → 1638',t:['cpu:vcc 3.0','1638','vcc 3.0']},
492	    {l:'works because clock.vcc is read live per conversion',t:['live','per conversion','clock.vcc','no re-arm','immediately']}],
493	 m:`ADC:REFERENCE AVCC          # unit of measure = the live supply rail
494	ADC:CHANNEL0:INJECT 1.2     # fixed input
495	ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?           # expect int(1.2/3.3 × 4095) = 1489
496	CPU:VCC 3.0                 # simulate the brown-out / battery sag
497	ADC:CHANNEL0:RAW?           # expect int(1.2/3.0 × 4095) = 1638
498	The second read changes with NO re-arming or re-selection because _adc_reference_voltage() reads self.clock.vcc at every conversion — AVCC is live, not a snapshot. The code RISES as the supply FALLS (shorter ruler, higher tick mark), which is exactly the inverse-transfer-function trick supply-monitoring firmware uses: measure a known voltage, infer the reference.`
499	},
